.TH BPFCD 8 "November 2026" "bpfc" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bpfcd \- keep bpfc programs attached to network interfaces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bpfcd
.RB [ \-n
.IR netns ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR config ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-D ]
.RB [ \-t ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bpfcd
compiles the program files under
.I /etc/bpfc/interfaces.d
with
.BR bpfc (8)
and keeps them attached to the TC ingress and egress hooks, and the XDP
hook, of the matching interfaces. It is normally started by systemd as
.BR bpfcd.service .
.SS File layout
.EX
/etc/bpfc/interfaces.d/<interface>/ingress/<priority>.bpfc
/etc/bpfc/interfaces.d/<interface>/egress/<priority>.bpfc
/etc/bpfc/interfaces.d/%all%/{ingress,egress}/<priority>.bpfc
/etc/bpfc/interfaces.d/%<kind>%/{ingress,egress}/<priority>.bpfc
/etc/bpfc/interfaces.d/{<interface>,%all%,%<kind>%}/xdp.bpfc
.EE
.PP
The priority is 1\-65535 without leading zeros; lower priorities run first.
Each file holds bpfc instructions separated by \(oq;\(cq. A \(oq#\(cq at the start of
an instruction begins a comment that ends at the end of the line. Files that
do not follow this naming are ignored with a warning; world-writable files are
refused, since a program can drop or redirect any traffic.
.SS XDP
.I xdp.bpfc
in an interface's directory is that interface's XDP program, compiled with
.B "bpfc \-d xdp"
and attached in generic mode. There is one per interface, so it has no
priority (shown as
.B xdp
with priority
.B \-
by
.BR bpfcctl ).
It runs before TC ingress: a packet it passes goes on to the ingress
programs. Templates, empty files, reloads, failures, adoption and external
changes work as for TC priorities; the kernel program is named
.IB iface _xdp_0 .
Commands that need an skb are refused at compile time (see
.BR bpfc (8)),
so a template shared between TC and XDP guards them with
.BR "when $XDP == 0" .
bpfcd notices an XDP program removed or replaced by something else from the
link notification, which the kernel sends only while the interface is up; a
change made while it is down is seen when it comes up or at the next reload.
.SS Start-up
Every file is compiled and attached. If the previous run's state is still in
.I /run/bpfc
and the kernel still has exactly that program at that priority from a file
with the same SHA-256, the program is adopted rather than reinstalled, so a
restart does not disturb traffic. Programs whose files were deleted while
.B bpfcd
was stopped are detached.
.SS Reload (SIGHUP)
.B systemctl reload bpfcd
(or
.BR "bpfcctl reload" ,
which waits for the result) rereads
.I bpfc.conf
and rescans the directory:
.IP \(bu 2
A file is hashed only if its modification time changed, and reinstalled only
if the hash differs too. Touching a file therefore does not reinstall it.
.IP \(bu
A program that uses
.B include
is hashed together with every file it includes (as listed by
.BR "bpfc \-M" )
on every rescan, so editing a shared include file reinstalls the programs
that use it. Keep include files outside
.I interfaces.d
(for example in
.IR /etc/bpfc/include ),
where bpfcd does not treat them as programs. World-writable include files
are refused, like world-writable programs.
.IP \(bu
A changed program replaces the old one atomically at the same priority, so
packets are never left unfiltered during the swap. (If the filter at that
priority has a different protocol or classifier, the kernel cannot swap them
atomically and bpfc removes the old one first.)
.IP \(bu
A deleted file's program is detached; other priorities and the other
direction are untouched.
.IP \(bu
Programs in any non-installed state (failed, changed externally, reinstall
limit) are retried. This is how to retry after fixing something outside the
file itself, such as a missing redirect target.
.SS Failures
If bpfc cannot compile a file, the kernel verifier rejects it, or attaching
fails, the priority is left
.B empty
\(em an older version of the program is never left running in its place. The
slot shows as
.B failed
in
.BR bpfcctl (8)
and the full bpfc output (including the verifier log) is kept in
.IR /run/bpfc/errors/ ,
shown by
.BR "bpfcctl show" .
.SS All interfaces and interface kinds
.I %all%/
applies to every interface.
.IR %<kind>%/ ,
for example
.IR %veth%/ ,
.IR %gre%/ ,
.I %vxlan%/
or
.IR %wireguard%/ ,
applies to every interface of that kind as
.B ip -d link
shows it;
.I %nic%/
is for devices without a kind (physical NICs) and
.I %lo%/
for loopback. On devices without an Ethernet header (gre, ipip, sit,
wireguard, tun) packets start with the IP header; bpfc knows this from the
device type and reads the IP fields at offset 0. A template that uses
Ethernet fields (MACs, VLAN, ARP, MPLS) fails to compile on such a device
(status
.BR failed ,
the others still get it): guard that part with
.B "when $L3OFF == 14"
or put an empty file for that priority in the kind's directory.
.PP
For each direction and priority the most specific file wins: the
interface's own directory, then its kind, then
.IR %all% .
Other priorities still apply. A file that is empty (or holds only comments)
means nothing at that priority on that interface; the slot shows as
.BR excluded .
A template is compiled once per interface; bpfc sets
.BR $IFNAME ,
.BR $IFINDEX ,
.B $IFTYPE
and
.B $IFKIND
for it (see
.BR bpfc (8)).
Interfaces created later get the programs when they appear; when they are
deleted, their template slots are dropped. Stacked devices (a bridge and its
ports, a bond and its slaves) each run the program, so a packet can be seen
more than once.
.SS Interfaces
A netlink monitor watches interfaces. When a configured interface appears
(or is deleted and created again), its programs are installed. When it is
removed, its programs are marked
.B pending-iface
until it returns. If a managed interface is renamed, the programs are removed
from it, because the configuration is keyed by name.
.SS External changes
The monitor also watches TC filters on managed interfaces. If a program
.B bpfcd
installed is removed or replaced by anything else (for example
.BR tc ,
a manual
.B bpfc
run, or deleting the clsact qdisc), the
.B on_external_change
setting decides whether it is reinstalled or only logged; see
.BR bpfc.conf (5).
Filters at priorities that have no file are never touched. A filter at a
priority that does have a file is replaced, with a warning.
.SS Identification
Each filter is named
.BI bpfcd: iface / direction / priority
(visible in
.BR "tc filter show" ),
and each program gets the kernel name
.IB iface _ direction _ priority
(its last 15 characters, visible in
.BR "bpftool prog" ).
The program id and tag bpfc reported are recorded in
.I /run/bpfc/state
next to the file path and hash.
.SS Network namespaces
One
.B bpfcd
manages one network namespace. Started with
.BI \-n " name"
it manages namespace
.I name
instead of the one it was started in, with its own configuration:
.EX
/etc/bpfc/netns/<name>/bpfc.conf
/etc/bpfc/netns/<name>/interfaces.d/<interface>/ingress/<priority>.bpfc
.EE
.PP
Everything described above (templates, XDP, reload, external changes)
works the same inside the namespace. Run one instance per namespace, usually as
.BR bpfcd-netns@<name>.service ,
next to
.B bpfcd.service
for the host.
.PP
.B Configuration.
If
.I /etc/bpfc/netns/<name>/bpfc.conf
does not exist,
.I /etc/bpfc/bpfc.conf
supplies the behavior settings (on_external_change, reinstall_limit,
remove_on_stop, compile_timeout, log_level, bpfc_path) but never its paths,
which belong to the host's bpfcd. Paths the namespace's file does not set
default to
.IR /etc/bpfc/netns/<name>/interfaces.d ,
.I /run/bpfc/netns/<name>
(state) and
.I /sys/fs/bpf/netns/<name>
(pinned maps). Maps get their own directory because bpffs is shared by every
namespace: two namespaces using the same map names would otherwise share
their contents. bpfcd creates the map directory.
.PP
.B Finding the namespace.
By default the namespace is
.IR /run/netns/<name> ,
as created by
.BR "ip netns add" .
Two settings in the namespace's
.I bpfc.conf
replace that (see
.BR bpfc.conf (5)):
.TP 4
.BI netns_path " = " /path
A namespace file such as
.I /run/netns/x
or
.IR /proc/<pid>/ns/net ,
or a symbolic link to one. Symlinks, chains of them included, are followed
again on every check, so whatever creates the namespace can point the link at
a new one.
.TP
.BI netns_veth " = " ifname
The host side of a veth pair: the namespace is whichever one holds its peer,
such as a pod or container. bpfcd finds it through a
.I /run/netns
entry or a process inside it, so no name has to be registered anywhere.
.PP
.B Following it.
bpfcd checks the namespace every second.
.IP \(bu 2
Not there (yet): bpfcd starts anyway, shows its programs as
.B pending-iface
and waits. Nothing is installed in the namespace bpfcd started in.
.IP \(bu
Gone (the path no longer exists, the veth was deleted, or nothing but bpfcd
is left in it): the programs are left as they are and bpfcd stops holding
the namespace, so the kernel can free it. It waits for it to come back.
.IP \(bu
Replaced (the path or veth now leads to a different namespace): bpfcd
removes its programs from the old namespace and installs them in the new one.
.PP
A reload (SIGHUP) also looks for a missing namespace at once. A restarted
bpfcd adopts the programs still running only if the namespace is the one in
its saved state.
.PP
.B Tools.
.BR bpfcctl (8)
and the bpfc-scripts tools take
.BI \-n " name"
too.
.B bpfcctl \-n
.I name
.B exec
.I command
runs a command inside the namespace with its paths in
.BR BPFC_ *
variables.
.SS REST API
With
.B rest_socket
set in
.BR bpfc.conf (5)
(it is off by default), bpfcd serves HTTP/1.1 on that unix socket. The socket
gets the owner and mode of
.B rest_socket_owner
and
.B rest_socket_mode
(default
.B root:bpfc
and
.BR 0660 ).
That is the only access control: whoever can connect can read and change
programs, and so load programs into the kernel as root. Add users to the
.B bpfc
group to let them in. Requests and replies are JSON
.RB ( "Content-Type: application/json" ).
.PP
Programs written through the socket are files in
.B rest_dir
.RI ( /var/lib/bpfc/interfaces.d ,
or
.I /var/lib/bpfc/netns/<name>/interfaces.d
with
.BR \-n ),
laid out like
.IR /etc/bpfc/interfaces.d .
bpfcd reads both trees. A file in
.B rest_dir
overrides the file at the same place in
.I /etc/bpfc/interfaces.d
(same interface or template, direction and priority); the precedence
between an interface, its kind and
.B %all%
then applies as usual. The API never changes
.IR /etc/bpfc .
Files in
.B rest_dir
stay in effect when the socket is turned off.
.TP
.B GET /
The daemon, a summary and every program, like
.BR "bpfcctl status" .
.TP
.B GET /interfaces
Every interface, and every template with files or programs.
.TP
.BI "GET /interfaces/" if
One interface (or
.BR %all% ,
.BI % kind %\fR):
whether it exists, its ifindex, kind, MTU, flags and MAC address, its
programs and its files.
.TP
.BI "GET /interfaces/" if / dir
The same for one direction
.RB ( ingress " or " egress ).
.TP
.BI "GET /interfaces/" if / dir / prio
The program file: a JSON array with one string per line. The
.B X\-Bpfc\-File
and
.B X\-Bpfc\-Source
.RB ( rest " or " config )
headers say which file it is. 404 when neither tree has it.
.TP
.BI "POST /interfaces/" if / dir / prio
Writes the body, a JSON array of strings, one per line, as the file in
.B rest_dir
and installs it.
.B PUT
is the same. The program is checked first with
.B bpfc \-T
(compiled and verified by the kernel, nothing attached) on one interface it
applies to; if the check fails, nothing changes and the reply is 422 with
bpfc's output. If installing still fails somewhere, the previous file (or no
file) is put back and installed again, and the reply is 422 with the failure
output. The program at that priority is briefly absent while that happens. A
write rescans both trees, as a reload does. An empty array writes an empty
file, which excludes the priority on that interface. 201 for a new file,
otherwise 200.
.TP
.BI "POST /interfaces/" if / dir / prio ?dry\-run
Checks the program on every interface it would apply to, and changes
nothing.
.TP
.BI "DELETE /interfaces/" if / dir / prio
Removes the file from
.B rest_dir
(and the program, or the
.I /etc/bpfc/interfaces.d
file it overrode comes back). 409 for a file that is only in
.IR /etc/bpfc/interfaces.d .
.TP
.BI /interfaces/ if /xdp
The interface's XDP program file, with the same methods.
.PP
Write
.B %all%
and
.BI % kind %
as
.B %25all%25
and
.BI %25 kind %25
in URLs (a literal
.B %
works too when it is not followed by two hex digits). Errors are
.BR "{\(dqerror\(dq: {\(dqstatus\(dq: N, \(dqmessage\(dq: \(dq...\(dq}}" .
Each connection carries one request; bodies need a
.B Content\-Length
(at most 1 MiB; no chunked bodies); a request must arrive within 10 seconds;
32 connections at a time. bpfcd handles one request at a time, and a write
waits for bpfc, so requests can wait up to
.B compile_timeout
behind one another. Writes are logged with the client's uid and pid.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
.RS
curl \-\-unix\-socket /run/bpfc/bpfcd.sock http://localhost/interfaces/eth0
curl \-\-unix\-socket /run/bpfc/bpfcd.sock \-X POST \e
    \-d '["match tcp;", "  match tcp\-dst 23;", "    drop"]' \e
    http://localhost/interfaces/eth0/ingress/10
.RE
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n ", " \-\-netns " name"
Manage network namespace
.IR name ;
see
.BR "Network namespaces" .
Names are 1-64 characters of
.BR A\-Z " " a\-z " " 0\-9 " " _ " " . " " \- .
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-config " file"
Configuration file (default
.IR /etc/bpfc/bpfc.conf ,
or the namespace's with
.BR \-n ).
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-background
Detach and log to syslog, for systems without systemd. Without it,
.B bpfcd
stays in the foreground and logs to stderr (with journal priorities when run
by systemd).
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-debug
Log debug messages.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-test
Check the configuration file and list the programs that would be installed,
without touching the kernel.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH CONTAINERS
The
.B bpfc-container
package provides
.BR bpfcd-entrypoint ,
a container entrypoint that checks the container's root user and
capabilities (\fBCAP_NET_ADMIN\fR, and \fBCAP_BPF\fR or \fBCAP_SYS_ADMIN\fR),
makes sure the map directory is on a bpffs mount (mounting a private one
at \fI/sys/fs/bpf\fR if it has to), raises the memlock limit, and then
replaces itself with
.B bpfcd
in the foreground. bpfcd then runs as PID 1: it receives the runtime's stop
and kill signals directly and reaps orphaned processes. Other commands given
to the entrypoint, such as
.BR "bpfcctl status" ,
run in place of the daemon. See
.I /usr/share/doc/bpfc-container/README.md
for run options and an example Containerfile.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BI BPFCD_ SETTING
Any setting from
.BR bpfc.conf (5),
named in capitals (for example
.BR BPFCD_ON_EXTERNAL_CHANGE=log ),
overrides the file. Read again on reload.
.BR bpfcctl (8)
reads the same variables.
.TP
.B NOTIFY_SOCKET
When set (by systemd, or by podman with
.BR \-\-sdnotify=container ),
bpfcd reports readiness and status through it.
.TP
.B BPFCD_NETNS
(container entrypoint only) the same as
.BR \-n .
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload, as described above.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop. Programs stay attached unless
.B remove_on_stop
is set.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/bpfc/bpfc.conf
Settings.
.TP
.I /etc/bpfc/interfaces.d/
Program files.
.TP
.I /var/lib/bpfc/interfaces.d/
Program files written through the REST socket (rest_dir).
.TP
.I /run/bpfc/state
Current state, read by
.BR bpfcctl (8).
.TP
.I /run/bpfc/errors/
bpfc output of failed programs.
.TP
.I /run/bpfc/bpfcd.pid
PID file, locked while bpfcd runs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bpfcctl (8),
.BR bpfc.conf (5),
.BR bpfc (8),
.BR tc (8)
